    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the primary differences in thermal dissipation rates between carbon-ceramic and semi-metallic brake pads during repeated high-speed deceleration cycles? Specifically, I'm looking at the heat soak effect and how it impacts the friction coefficient stability.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The primary difference in thermal dissipation rates between carbon-ceramic and semi-metallic pads during repeated high-speed deceleration cycles is quite significant, as the heat management is where they diverge most sharply. Carbon-ceramic pads, by their nature, handle extreme thermal loads much more effectively; they dissipate heat more rapidly and maintain a lower overall operating temperature compared to semi-metallic pads. This superior heat rejection in carbon-ceramic directly translates to a much more stable friction coefficient, even through repeated cycles, because the pad material resists the thermal breakdown that causes fade.\n\nSemi-metallic pads, while offering excellent initial bite, tend to absorb and retain more thermal energy—the \"heat soak effect\" is more pronounced. As they heat up repeatedly, the organic or metallic components can begin to experience thermal degradation, leading to volatile outgassing and a noticeable drop in the friction coefficient, which is the classic brake fade. In essence, the thermal inertia of the semi-metallic pad is higher, making it more susceptible to the cumulative heat stress of repeated high-speed stops.\n\nThink of it this way: carbon-ceramic is like a highly efficient, heat-resistant metal sink, rapidly channeling away the thermal energy, while the semi-metallic pad is more like a",
      "source": "target",
